{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nBoth review gates execute criterion work through runners whose return values cannot reliably distinguish clean execution from failed, cancelled, skipped, budget-shed, or resumed work. Plan-review failures can become empty successful checkpoints; code review loses earlier successful work when a later criterion raises. An effective-policy snapshot alone cannot fix those execution facts.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a narrow shared discovery kernel as a NEW `discovery` submodule under `rebar.llm.review_kernel`, independent of that package's four-pass DECISION kernel (find/verify/decide/coach): it neither imports from nor modifies `verify`/`decide`/`coach`/`verify_models`, and `__init__` gains additive exports only.\n\nDefine immutable `DiscoveryStagePlan`/`DiscoveryUnitPlan` inputs plus `DiscoveryStageResult` and typed per-unit outcomes (`success`, `resumed`, `skipped`, `shed`, `failed`, `cancelled`). A plan carries stable unit ids, dependencies, prompt/contract/model/mode identities, context and policy digests, blocking capability, budget estimate, and gate-supplied execution callbacks.\n\nThe executor owns dependency-aware scheduling, explicit positive-budget shedding (deterministic order: ascending dependency rank, then stable unit id, so the shed set is identical across runs; shedding a unit also sheds its unmet dependents as `skipped`), usage accumulation, cancellation observation, and success-only checkpoint envelope creation. After RP-01 exhausts a local operation it preserves successful independent results and continues; a dependent of missing work becomes `skipped` with a dependency reason. Systemic configuration/provider initialization failures and cancellation abort remaining execution with exact completed-so-far accounting. Gate verdicts remain outside the executor.\n\nCancellation reuses the existing cut point: `plan_review.generation.review_cancelled()` is observed BEFORE dispatch, and an already-dispatched provider call is non-interruptible (sunk cost). The kernel formalizes that boundary — a unit dispatched before cancellation is observed runs to completion and commits as its true outcome (`success` on digest-complete content, `failed` on error) with real usage counted; a not-yet-dispatched unit is `cancelled` and never started. Completed work is never dropped or relabelled, so AC3's accounting is total.\n\nDefine a new versioned, digest-complete checkpoint/trace envelope whose identity covers material, code/ref, effective-policy snapshot, prompt and contract, model/mode, topology, input context, dependencies, and namespace version. Only `success` and content-identical `resumed` units may commit as reusable successes; failed, cancelled, skipped, and shed units remain missing. Legacy checkpoints are never read as new successes.\n\nThe typed trace is safe stored diagnostic data — normalized ids, outcomes, reason codes, usage, lineage only; it excludes raw prompts, ticket/context bodies, provider responses, and secrets. No per-unit trace is added to review or review-status response contracts.\n\n## Boundaries and Rationale\nKeep policy selection, gate context construction, YAML/BPMN topology, provider-turn retry/output handling, checkpoint persistence, and final verdict decisions in their current owners.\n\nPrior-art reconciliation — the kernel defines unified typed successors that RP-06's migration stories move the gates onto; current implementations stay unchanged until then:\n- Budget: `sizing.shed_to_budget` treats `cap_override=None` as the centrality-scaled COMPUTED cap (not uncapped), an explicit `0.0` as a valid \"shed everything\" cap, and never sheds container (G3/G4) criteria. The kernel deliberately defines a simpler typed contract (positive cap sheds by (rank, id); omission uncapped; zero rejected). S3 owns mapping `REBAR_PLAN_REVIEW_BUDGET`/`cap_override` and container-never-shed on explicitly, so no cost posture changes silently here.\n- Checkpoint/resume: `sizing.load_checkpoint`/`save_checkpoint`/`_checkpoint_key` and `pass1.run_pass1` are chunk-keyed and can round-trip an empty-but-\"successful\" checkpoint; the kernel's versioned digest-complete envelope supersedes that keying, existing functions remaining until S3.\n- Usage/preservation: `run_pass1` already extends findings independently and aggregates usage; the kernel restates that as typed outcomes so a later-raising unit cannot erase earlier successes.\n- Cancellation: reused verbatim from `generation.review_cancelled()` (Approach); no new mechanism.\n\nThe per-unit dependency graph and `skipped`-with-dependency-reason are introduced as the shared-substrate contract, NOT because a current gate exhibits such an edge: today plan-review Pass-1 chunks/agent criteria fan out INDEPENDENTLY (a failed chunk merely counted in `chunk_errors`) and code-review runs each criterion independently — so for both current gates the graph is EMPTY and scheduling is independent fan-out. The typed edge exists so migration stories composing a unit that consumes another's result express a skip-on-missing dependency as a first-class `skipped` outcome instead of a silently-emptied success. Introducing it now, with the empty-graph path tested, keeps the envelope's dependency digest (AC5) stable across migrations.\n\n## Testing\nIn-process contract tests with the real plan/result/envelope codecs and an injected stateful fake only at the model-call boundary, asserting exact call order, outcomes, usage, checkpoint eligibility, collateral state, and a clean retry after injected local failure. Negative controls prove a systemic failure aborts and a failed/shed/cancelled result never round-trips as success. Covered: the empty-dependency-graph fan-out path; a cancellation where an in-flight call completing after the signal commits as its true outcome while a not-yet-dispatched sibling is `cancelled`; a budget case with an identical shed set across runs, zero rejected, omission uncapped.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `DiscoveryStagePlan` and `DiscoveryStageResult` represent stable unit identity, dependencies, blocking capability, usage, and all six outcome kinds without gate-specific verdict fields.\n- [ ] The discovery contracts live in a new `rebar.llm.review_kernel.discovery` submodule with additive `__init__` exports and make no change to the existing `verify`/`decide`/`coach`/`verify_models` symbols.\n- [ ] Given one exhausted local failure, the real executor retains earlier successes, executes later independent units, and marks dependent units skipped with the missing dependency id; an empty dependency graph degenerates to independent fan-out.\n- [ ] Given a systemic initialization/configuration failure or cancellation, the executor stops scheduling new work and returns exact completed, failed, and cancelled accounting without fabricating usage or findings; a unit whose non-interruptible call was already dispatched when cancellation is observed commits as its true `success`/`failed` outcome with real usage, while a not-yet-dispatched unit is `cancelled`.\n- [ ] A positive explicit budget deterministically sheds work that does not fit — in ascending (dependency rank, unit id) order so the shed set is identical across runs; omission is uncapped and zero is rejected rather than interpreted as disablement. This deliberately supersedes `sizing.shed_to_budget`, whose semantics are unchanged until S3 maps them on.\n- [ ] The versioned envelope round-trips only digest-complete, content-identical success/resume records; injected failed, cancelled, skipped, shed, corrupt, and legacy envelopes remain missing.\n- [ ] Stored trace serialization contains normalized outcome metadata and lineage but no raw prompt, ticket/context body, provider payload, or secret, and public review result schemas remain unchanged.\n- [ ] Fault-injection tests prove restoration plus a clean rerun succeeds and unrelated success state is unchanged."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787347554412131001,"uuid":"a1544fda-0108-46c5-ae0c-3d316dd0b011"}